<header><a href="/">MAE Academy</a><nav aria-label="Primary"><a href="/simulations">Simulations</a> <a href="/research">Research</a> <a href="/library">Library</a> <a href="/about">About</a> <a href="/contact">Contact</a></nav></header>
<main>
<h1>Control Systems</h1>
<article lang="en">
<header><h2>PID Controller</h2><p>A PID controller combines proportional, integral and derivative action on the error signal. It remains the most widely deployed feedback law in industrial automation because each term has a clear physical meaning.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and meaning</h3><p>This tool closes a feedback loop around the plant transfer function you enter, using a P, I, D, PI, PD or PID controller in one of several forms. It simulates the response to the selected reference signal and reports overshoot, peak time, settling time and steady-state error, so you can see directly what each gain does.</p></section>
<section><h3>Mathematical model</h3>
<h4>Parallel and ISA standard forms</h4><p>In the parallel form the control signal is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(t) = K_p e(t) + K_i \int_0^t e(\tau)d\tau + K_d\,de(t)/dt</annotation></semantics></math></span> with independent gains. The standard ISA form factors out the proportional gain, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s) = K_p\left(1 + 1/(T_i s) + T_d s/\left((T_d/N)s + 1\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so that <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> are times. The two forms are equivalent when <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i = K_p/T_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_d = K_p T_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
<h4>Derivative filter</h4><p>A pure derivative is not realisable and amplifies measurement noise. The filter coefficient <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> adds a pole at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s = -N/T_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> and limits the high-frequency gain of the controller to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_p(1 + N)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Typical values lie between <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and a larger <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives a faster derivative at the price of more noise.</p>
<h4>Integrator anti-windup</h4><p>When the actuator saturates, the integral state keeps growing and later causes a long overshoot. Clamping freezes the integrator whenever the unsaturated command <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math></span> lies outside <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[u_{min}, u_{max}]</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the error has the same sign as <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The integrator resumes as soon as the error drives the command back into range.</p>
<h4>Transient metrics</h4><p>For a dominant second-order closed loop with damping ratio <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math></span> and natural frequency <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_n</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the percent overshoot is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_p = e^{-\pi\zeta/\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}} \times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the two percent settling time is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_s \approx 4/(\zeta\omega_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The rise time is approximated by <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_r \approx (\pi - \arccos\zeta)/(\omega_n\sqrt{1-\zeta^2})</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Raising <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_p</annotation></semantics></math></span> shortens <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_r</annotation></semantics></math></span>, adding <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> removes steady-state error to a step, and adding <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> increases damping.</p>
<h4>Governing equations</h4>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(t) = K_p\,e(t) + K_i \int_0^t e(\tau)\,d\tau + K_d \frac{de(t)}{dt}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Parallel PID control law in the time domain.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s) = K_p\left(1 + \frac{1}{T_i s} + \frac{T_d s}{\frac{T_d}{N}s + 1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>ISA standard form with derivative filter coefficient <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i = \frac{K_p}{T_i},\qquad K_d = K_p T_d,\qquad p_f = -\frac{N}{T_d}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Conversion between standard and parallel gains, and the filter pole.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(s) = \frac{C(s)\,G(s)}{1 + C(s)\,G(s)}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Closed-loop transfer function with the controller <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and plant <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">{</mo><mtable rowspacing="0.36em" columnalign="left left" columnspacing="1em"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>or</mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mi>e</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">sat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}_I = \begin{cases} K_i\,e(t) &amp; u_{min} &lt; v &lt; u_{max} \;\text{or}\; e\,v &lt; 0 \\ 0 &amp; \text{otherwise} \end{cases},\qquad u = \operatorname{sat}(v)</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Anti-windup by conditional integration (clamping) with actuator saturation.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_p = e^{-\frac{\pi\zeta}{\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}}} \times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Percent overshoot as a function of the damping ratio.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_s \approx \frac{4}{\zeta\omega_n},\qquad t_p = \frac{\pi}{\omega_n\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Settling time and peak time for a second-order response.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_r \approx \frac{\pi - \arccos\zeta}{\omega_n\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Rise time approximation for a second-order response.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section><h3>Canvas parameter and control mapping</h3><dl>
<dt>Plant transfer function</dt><dd>The numerator and denominator you enter define <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The closed loop is built as <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s)G(s)/(1 + C(s)G(s))</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt>Controller Architecture: P, I, D, PI, PD, PID</dt><dd>Selects which terms are active. Only the gain sliders that apply to the chosen structure are shown, for example <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> is hidden for P and PD.</dd>
<dt>Controller Form</dt><dd>Chooses between the parallel, series, parallel standard, parallel modified, series standard and series modified forms, which change how <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_p</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> interact.</dd>
<dt>Gain sliders <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_p</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt><dd>Set the controller coefficients. The defaults are <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_p = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_d = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> limited to the range from <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt>Reference signal</dt><dd>Impulse, step, ramp, parabolic, exponential, sine or cosine, with a magnitude and a parameter. Ramp and parabolic inputs expose steady-state error of higher system types.</dd>
<dt>Interactive scope</dt><dd>Plots <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> over a horizon of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math></span> seconds, starting at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt>Design by Specs</dt><dd>Takes a target <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the range <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and a target <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_n</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and proposes gains for the second-order specification.</dd>
<dt>Transient Metrics card</dt><dd>Reports the measured overshoot, peak time, settling time and steady-state error from the simulated response.</dd>
</dl></section>
<section><h3>Worked numerical example</h3><h4>Converting ISA settings and predicting the response</h4><p>An ISA controller uses <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_p = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_i = 0.5\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_d = 0.1\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find the parallel gains and the filter pole, then predict the transient metrics when the closed loop behaves as a second-order system with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mtext> rad/s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_n = 4\text{ rad/s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><ol>
<li><p>Convert to parallel gains using <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i = K_p/T_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_d = K_p T_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>0.5</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i = \frac{2}{0.5} = 4,\qquad K_d = 2 \times 0.1 = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The derivative filter pole sits at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-N/T_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the high-frequency gain is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_p(1 + N)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>0.1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> rad/s</mtext><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_f = -\frac{10}{0.1} = -100\text{ rad/s},\qquad K_p(1+N) = 2 \times 11 = 22</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The percent overshoot for <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> uses <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.866</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{1 - 0.25} = 0.866</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mn>0.866</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">%</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.814</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">%</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>16.3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_p = e^{-\frac{\pi \times 0.5}{0.866}} \times 100\% = e^{-1.814} \times 100\% \approx 16.3\%</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The settling time and peak time follow from <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\omega_n = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the damped frequency <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.866</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3.464</mn><mtext> rad/s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_d = 4 \times 0.866 = 3.464\text{ rad/s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> s</mtext><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>3.464</mn></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.907</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_s \approx \frac{4}{2} = 2\text{ s},\qquad t_p = \frac{\pi}{3.464} \approx 0.907\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Because <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.047</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arccos 0.5 = 1.047</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the rise time approximation gives:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1.047</mn></mrow><mn>3.464</mn></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.605</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_r \approx \frac{\pi - 1.047}{3.464} \approx 0.605\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
</ol><p>The parallel gains are <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i = 4</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_d = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the filter pole is at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> rad/s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-100\text{ rad/s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the loop is expected to show <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>16.3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_p \approx 16.3\%</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_s \approx 2\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.907</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_p \approx 0.907\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.605</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_r \approx 0.605\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></section>
<section><h3>Industrial applications and standards</h3><dl>
<dt>Motor drives</dt><dd>Speed and current loops of servo drives, where anti-windup protects against voltage and current limits during acceleration.</dd>
<dt>Process industry</dt><dd>Temperature, flow and level control, where PI action dominates and the derivative term is used sparingly with a strong filter.</dd>
<dt>Robotics</dt><dd>Joint position control, where the derivative term supplies damping and the filter coefficient <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> limits encoder noise.</dd>
<dt>Aerospace</dt><dd>Attitude and altitude hold loops that use gain scheduling around PID controllers across the flight envelope.</dd>
<dt>Energy systems</dt><dd>Inverter and converter regulators, as well as turbine speed governors, tuned for a specified overshoot and settling time.</dd>
</dl></section>
</article>
